{
 "_comment": [
  "Shared fixture of the photo colour fit (spec 2026-10-13 section 4.3, weighted by spec 2026-11-02 section 4.1): cc_probe.fit_ccm (Python) and fitColourMatrix (Dart, operator app) must both reproduce it. Written by fixtures/make_colour_fit_cases.py, which computes every expected value with an independent closed form, never with fit_ccm.",
  "Inputs per case: card ('spyder24' or 'classic') and version ('' or 'pre2014'); measured_srgb8 = 24 x 3 floats, R,G,B order, sRGB 0..255 as the camera encoded them (patch means; not necessarily integers), in the JSON patch order of references/<card>.json (Spyder: 1E..6E, 1F..6F, 1G..6G, 1H..6H; Classic: 1..24); usable = 24 bools: the patches that enter the least squares and the score; weights = 24 numbers, the reference's weights (skin patches 3, the others 1: 5H and 6H on the Spyder, 1 and 2 on the Classic); grey_index = the card's exposure grey (3E = 2 on the Spyder, patch 21 = 20 on the Classic). The reference = that JSON's lab values (Lab D50) in that version.",
  "1. lin = srgb_to_linear(measured / 255), cc_probe's curve (OpenCV ccm's sRGBBase_), NOT the textbook 0.04045 / 12.92 one: a = 0.055, gamma = 2.4, alpha = 1 + a, K0 = a / (gamma - 1) = 0.03928571428571429, phi = (alpha^gamma * (gamma - 1)^(gamma - 1)) / (a^(gamma - 1) * gamma^gamma) = 12.923210180787855 (the continuous linear segment); lin = ((|v| + a) / alpha)^gamma for v > K0, -((|v| + a) / alpha)^gamma for v < -K0, else v / phi. (Its inverse, linear_to_srgb, used only to build the 'exact' case: beta = K0 / phi = 0.003039934639778432.) The values are in colour_constants.",
  "2. ref = lab_d50_to_linear_srgb(ref_lab): Lab D50 -> XYZ D50 (white_d50 = 0.9642119944211994, 1, 0.8251882845188288; delta = 6/29, f^-1(f) = f^3 above delta, else 3 delta^2 (f - 4/29)) -> M_D50_TO_D65 -> XYZ D65 -> inv(M_RGB_TO_XYZ) -> linear sRGB. Every matrix is BUILT from the constants below in double precision, never pasted from a published rounded table (Lindbloom's Bradford or the usual 0.4124 sRGB matrix miss the 1e-6 tolerance by ~1e-5 on the matrix and up to ~9e-4 on the worst dE). M_RGB_TO_XYZ: for each primary (x, y) the column (x/y, 1, (1-x-y)/y) with primaries R 0.64/0.33, G 0.30/0.60, B 0.15/0.06; then each column scaled by S = solve(columns, white_d65), white_d65 = 0.95047, 1, 1.08883. Bradford: cone matrix B = [[0.8951, 0.2664, -0.1614], [-0.7502, 1.7135, 0.0367], [0.0389, -0.0685, 1.0296]]; M_D65_TO_D50 = inv(B) . diag((B . white_d50) / (B . white_d65)) . B and M_D50_TO_D65 = inv(M_D65_TO_D50) (equal to inv(B) . diag((B . white_d65) / (B . white_d50)) . B).",
  "3. Exposure out: Y(v) = 0.2126 R + 0.7152 G + 0.0722 B; lin *= Y(ref[grey_index]) / Y(lin[grey_index]).",
  "4. A = lin[usable] (n x 3), W = diag(weights[usable]). For each output channel k (row k of M), t = ref[usable, k]: the minimum of sum w_i (A_i . m_k - t_i)^2 with the row summing to 1 (white stays white), i.e. the solution of [[2 A'WA, 1], [1', 0]] [m_k; lambda] = [2 A'W t; 1] (4 x 4). The closed form that makes the fixture: substitute m_k3 = 1 - m_k1 - m_k2, then np.linalg.lstsq on the sqrt(w)-scaled rows of X = [A1 - A3, A2 - A3] against y = t - A3. M is row-major, RGB, linear light: corrected = M . lin. expected_matrix is the weighted one; expected_matrix_unweighted the same with every weight 1.",
  "5. dE before = dE2000(lab(lin), ref_lab) over ALL 24 patches, on the exposure-normalised lin of step 3 (ruling R3); dE after = dE2000(lab(lin @ M'), ref_lab) over all 24; lab() = linear sRGB -> M_RGB_TO_XYZ -> XYZ D65 -> M_D65_TO_D50 -> XYZ D50 -> Lab D50 against white_d50 (f(t) = cbrt t above (6/29)^3, else t / (3 (6/29)^2) + 4/29, so negative values stay finite; L = 116 fy - 16, a = 500 (fx - fy), b = 200 (fy - fz)). The mean and the worst (max) of the 24 values stay UNWEIGHTED and over all 24 patches (expected_mean_de_*, expected_worst_*). The score is the weighted mean over the USABLE patches only, sum(w dE) / sum(w): expected_score_before (on the exposure-normalised values) and expected_score_after; the correction is refused when score_after is not below score_before. dE2000 = Sharma, Wu, Dalal 2005, kL = kC = kH = 1.",
  "Cases: 'exact' = linear_to_srgb((ref @ inv(M_true)') * 0.8) * 255, unquantised (1F's red is negative), all usable. Its expected matrix is within 5e-4 of M_true, not equal to it: 3E is not neutral (a* +0.69, b* +1.86), so inv(M_true) changes its Y by ~1.8e-4 and step 3 rescales everything by that, which no row-sum-1 matrix can undo exactly (with a neutral 3E the fit returns M_true to 1e-12; cc_probe's test checks that). 'quantised' = 'exact' rounded to integers and clipped to 0..255; 'white clipped' = 'quantised' with patch 0 (1E) not usable. 'weighted spyder' = 'quantised' with the two skin patches (5H, 6H) seen through I + 0.6 (M_skin - I) (a warmer cast than the rest of the card): the unweighted fit spreads the error over all patches, the weighted one brings the skin closer to its reference at the others' cost. 'classic pre2014' = the ColorChecker Classic (version pre2014), measured = linear_to_srgb((ref @ inv(M_classic)') * 0.7) * 255 rounded to integers, exposure out on patch 21 (index 20); 'classic clipped white' = the same with patch 19 (index 18) clipped to 255,255,255 and not usable. 'skin made worse' = noise on every non-skin patch (numpy default_rng(192), sigma 12 % of the linear value), the two skin patches exact: the fit lowers the unweighted mean dE (expected_mean_de_after < expected_mean_de_before) but the weighted score does not improve (expected_refused); the skin guard keeps its weight-48 fit. 'skin kept at weight 6' = 'quantised' with the two skin patches seen through I + 2.5 (M_skin - I) (a stronger skin cast): with skin weight 3 the fit makes 5H more than 0.5 worse, with weight 6 it does not. 'skin worse at every weight' = the same with I - 0.1 (M_skin - I): even weight 48 makes a skin patch more than 0.5 worse while the score improves, so the skin refusal. 'the score improves only at weight 24' = noise on every patch (numpy default_rng(1), sigma 14 % of the linear value), then the two skin patches seen through I - 0.5 (M_skin - I): weights 3 and 6 make a skin patch more than 0.5 worse, weight 12 keeps the skin but does not lower the standard score, weight 24 does both (ruling F6: the guard goes on to 24 and the fit is saved).",
  "The skin guard (fitColourMatrixGuarded in Dart, cc_probe.fit_patches): the fit of step 4 with the skin patches weighted 3, then 6, 12, 24, 48 (the others 1); the first fit where every usable skin patch has dE after <= dE before + 0.5 AND the score at the standard weights (skin 3) goes down (ruling F6) is kept (expected_skin_weight), else the weight-48 fit. No case sits within 0.01 of the skin tolerance at any weight the guard tries, nor within 0.01 of the score before at a weight that keeps the skin (the script refuses to write such a case), so the outcome cannot flip on rounding. expected_guarded_matrix is the kept fit's matrix; expected_guarded_score_before / _after its scores recomputed with the STANDARD weights (skin 3) whatever weight the fit used, so runs compare. expected_refusal = the refusal of the kept fit, null when it can be saved: 'would not improve' (refusals[3]) when its standard score after is not below the score before, else 'skin worse' (refusals[4]) when a usable skin patch is worse by more than 0.5. expected_refused stays the plain weight-3 fit's own score check.",
  "Tolerances: matrix, dE and score values 1e-6 (Python checks 'exact' to 1e-9).",
  "refusals: the five refusal texts of the app and the tool (byte-identical), in the order they are checked: white clipped, patches too small, taps (app only), would not improve (after the fit: score after is not below score before), the skin colours worse (after the fit and the skin guard: a usable skin patch more than 0.5 dE2000 worse).",
  "colour_constants: the exact values cc_probe.py uses (SRGB_A, SRGB_GAMMA, _K0, _PHI, _BETA, SRGB_PRIMARIES, WHITE_D65, WHITE_D50, BRADFORD, _LAB_DELTA); test_cc_probe.py rebuilds cc_probe's matrices and curve from them to 1e-12."
 ],
